<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Lucerne</h1><p class="st-meta">1332 CE – 1798 CE · capital Lucerne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first city to ally with the Swiss forest cantons, in 1332, and later the leading Catholic power of the Confederacy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lucerne grew around the monastery of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leodegar">Leodegar</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reuss-river">Reuss</a> leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lucerne">Lake Lucerne</a>, and boomed after the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard</a> route in the 13th century. Sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1291, the town allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-uri">Canton of Uri</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Canton of Schwyz</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-unterwalden">Canton of Unterwalden</a> in 1332, and the Swiss victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Sempach</a> in 1386 freed it from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> rule.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small council of patrician families governed the city and a large rural territory acquired in the 15th century. Lucerne lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard</a> transit trade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> contracts. It led the Catholic cantons during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, hosted the papal nuncio from 1586, and with its allies crushed the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-peasant-war-of-1653">Peasants' War</a> of 1653.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The patrician regime abdicated in 1798 as French troops invaded, and Lucerne briefly served as capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. Restored as a canton in 1803, it remains a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/canton-of-lucerne.jpg" alt="Canton of Lucerne" width="320" height="361"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHE_Luzern_COA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1332 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucerne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lucerne, Luzern</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1565&amp;focus=canton-of-lucerne" aria-label="Canton of Lucerne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4875 526 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Canton of Lucerne lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5195 782L5186 782M5195 782L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5195 782h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5195 782h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lucerne. <a href="/atlas?year=1565&amp;focus=canton-of-lucerne">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
